  "answer_text": "The episode you are looking for is titled **\"Yuppy Love\"**. It is the first episode of **Series 6** (Season 6, Episode 1) and was first broadcast on **January 8, 1989**.\n\nWidely voted as the funniest moment in British television history, the \"falling through the bar\" scene is an iconic piece of physical comedy. \n\n### Context of the Scene:\nInspired by the film *Wall Street* and its protagonist Gordon Gekko, Del Boy (David Jason) decides to adopt a high-flying \"yuppy\" image\u2014complete with a Filofax, a silver briefcase, and red braces. To suit his new lifestyle, he ditches his usual pub, *The Nag's Head*, in favor of a trendy, upmarket London wine bar. \n\nDel and Trigger (Roger Lloyd Pack) are standing at the bar trying to chat up two attractive women across the room. \n\n### The Famous Moment:\nWanting to look slick and sophisticated, Del turns to Trigger and says:\n> **Del Boy:** *\"I think we're on a winner here, Trig, alright? Play it nice and cool, son, nice and cool. You know what I mean?\"*\n\nAs Del turns away to light a cigar and look coolly at the women, the barman lifts the bar flap (hatch) to step out, leaving it open. Del goes to lean casually back against the bar, not realizing the flap is gone, and plunges sideways right through the opening and onto the floor. \n\nThe comedy is heightened by Trigger's delayed reaction; he looks around in complete confusion, wondering where Del has suddenly vanished to. \n\nDel gets up, dusting himself off, and tries to keep his cool by saying:\n> **Del Boy:** *\"Drink up, Trig. Drink up, we're leaving.\"*\n> **Trigger:** *\"Ain't you gonna try for them birds?\"*\n> **Del Boy:** *\"No, no, you're cramping my style, mate.\"*\n\n### Other Highlights from this Episode:\nAside from this legendary scene, \"Yuppy Love\" is also a massive milestone for the show because **it is the episode where Rodney meets Cassandra** (his future wife) for the first time while attending an evening computer class.",
